---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Contact | elderabusecalc.com';
const description = 'How to reach the editorial team at elderabusecalc.com for corrections, citations, and interview requests. For active abuse concerns, contact APS or the Eldercare Locator hotline.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Contact</h1>

  <p>The editorial team welcomes corrections, citation suggestions, interview requests, and general feedback. We don&rsquo;t provide legal advice, don&rsquo;t review individual cases, and are not a substitute for Adult Protective Services.</p>

  <h2>If abuse is happening right now</h2>

  <p>Please do not email us with an active abuse concern &mdash; we are not equipped to respond quickly. Instead:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Emergency:</strong> call 911.</li>
    <li><strong>National Center on Elder Abuse / Eldercare Locator:</strong> <strong>1-800-677-1116</strong>. Connects you to your state&rsquo;s Adult Protective Services intake and other local resources.</li>
    <li><strong>State Long-Term Care Ombudsman:</strong> for nursing home or assisted living complaints. Find yours through the Eldercare Locator number above.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Editorial inquiries</h2>
  <p>Email <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a>. Please include the page URL when reporting a correction. We respond within a few business days.</p>

  <h2>What we can help with</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Corrections to published content (factual errors, outdated citations, broken links).</li>
    <li>Citation suggestions (peer-reviewed sources, recent appellate opinions, new state legislation).</li>
    <li>Interview requests from journalists or researchers.</li>
    <li>General feedback about the site.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we can&rsquo;t help with</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Legal advice on your specific case (contact an elder abuse attorney).</li>
    <li>Medical advice (contact a qualified provider).</li>
    <li>Active abuse reports (contact APS or call 1-800-677-1116).</li>
    <li>Specific case valuations beyond the calculator&rsquo;s general ranges.</li>
    <li>Case intake by email &mdash; this inbox doesn&rsquo;t process attorney referrals.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The homepage&rsquo;s case-review form is the disclosed way this site connects readers with attorneys &mdash; see our <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> for what&rsquo;s shared if you use it.</p>

  <h2>Before you email</h2>
  <p>Many questions we get are already answered on the Site: check the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> for common questions about reporting, timelines, and what counts as evidence, or the <a href="/guides/">guides</a> &mdash; including <a href="/guides/what-to-do-if-you-suspect-elder-abuse/">what to do if you suspect elder abuse</a>, <a href="/guides/recognizing-elder-abuse-and-nursing-home-neglect/">recognizing elder abuse and nursing home neglect</a>, and <a href="/guides/common-elder-abuse-claim-misconceptions/">common misconceptions about claims</a>. If your question is about how the calculator arrived at a number, the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a> walks through the damages categories and state-law enhancements in detail, with two worked examples.</p>

  <h2>If you actually need an attorney</h2>
  <p>This inbox itself doesn&rsquo;t process case intake or referrals &mdash; but if you have an active claim, the homepage includes an optional case-review form that connects you with attorneys. That&rsquo;s a disclosed advertisement, not this contact channel, and using it is always optional (see our <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> for what&rsquo;s shared if you use it). A few things are worth knowing before you search on your own, too. Elder abuse and nursing home neglect cases are a specialized practice area &mdash; look for attorneys or firms that specifically describe themselves as handling elder abuse, nursing home neglect, or long-term care litigation, rather than a general personal-injury practice, since these cases depend on facility survey records, F-Tag deficiency history, and state-specific statutory enhancements that a generalist may not know to pursue. Most elder abuse attorneys offer a free initial consultation and work on contingency (no upfront fee). Timing matters: nursing homes are not required to keep all internal records indefinitely, staffing and incident logs can be overwritten or purged on a routine schedule, and every state has its own statute of limitations, so waiting to consult an attorney can mean losing access to evidence even before a legal deadline is at issue. If you&rsquo;d rather start from a neutral referral source, your state bar association&rsquo;s lawyer referral service can point you to attorneys who handle elder abuse and nursing home cases in your area.</p>
  <p>If the situation involves financial exploitation specifically, speed matters even more: an attorney can pursue a civil restraining order or temporary conservatorship to stop further transfers, and many banks will place a hold on a suspicious transaction if it&rsquo;s reported quickly through the branch&rsquo;s fraud department or APS. The longer stolen funds stay in the wrongdoer&rsquo;s control, the more likely they are to be spent or moved somewhere a judgment can&rsquo;t reach.</p>
</BaseLayout>
